#!/usr/bin/env python
"""merge_steward.py — the merge queue, the train builder and the post-deploy verdict of this project.

Installed from ``templates/merge_steward.py.template`` to ``scripts/merge_steward.py`` and configured by
``config/delivery.json``. The CI host runs ``tick`` and ``verdict`` (``.github/workflows/merge-steward.yml``
and the deploy workflow's last job, or the GitLab ``merge-steward`` and ``delivery-verdict`` jobs); an
agent session runs only ``enqueue``, ``dequeue``, ``status`` and ``check-pr``, through the git wrapper.
``.claude/docs/delivery.md`` is the agent-facing contract; this docstring is the mechanism.

WHY IT EXISTS. Measured 2026-10-25 on a project with about 24 merges a day and a 55-minute CI: every merge
cancelled the trunk run of the merge before it (29 of 34 runs in one day), the deploy that only a green
trunk tip could start ran about three times a day and queued for 4.7 h, workers re-synced their branches
99 times in 31 sessions, and sessions sat waiting for a deploy of their OWN commit that supersession never
produced. A queue that tests the EXACT state it lands removes the race; verification by ANCESTRY (a green
post-deploy check on commit N verifies every landing N contains) removes the wait for one's own commit.

ONE TICK. Stateless: git (the train branches and their commit trailers) and the tracker (labels, comments)
hold all of the state, so a lost runner or a cancelled tick loses nothing.
  0. ``reverify``: an open issue labelled ``reverify`` asks for a fresh deploy of the trunk (the one way a
     session asks for a re-verification without owning the deploy); the label is consumed.
  1. Finish the batch in flight. Its trains are the branches ``<train_prefix><batch>-<k>``, k = 1..n, each
     the batch base (the trunk when it was planned) plus the batch's first k PRs. While any train's CI is
     still running, the tick stops there. A batch whose train the trunk already CONTAINS was pushed by a tick
     that stopped before its records were written: this tick completes them (every record is idempotent).
  2. Land. L = the largest k for which trains 1..k are all green, cut further at the first train whose PR
     moved on while it ran (its head is not the commit the train merged, it was dequeued, put on hold or
     closed): its new commits were never tested. The trunk is fast-forwarded to train L (a plain push: a
     moved trunk makes it fail, and then NOTHING lands — the PRs stay queued and the next tick rebuilds).
     Each landed PR and each issue it REFERENCES (``Refs #N``) gets the line
     ``merge-steward: landed <sha> (PR #<n>, ...)``; when a record cannot be written the trains stay and the
     next tick completes them. The culprit — train L+1's newest PR — leaves the queue with ``queue-failed``
     (or is re-queued ONCE when ``retry_culprit_once`` and it never failed a train before: a flaky suite must
     not bounce a good change); a train that could not run CI to a result (no run, cancelled, timed out) is
     retried once and blamed the second time. Later PRs stay queued. The trains are deleted and the deploy
     is started.
  3. Plan the next batch: the queued PRs in enqueue order, each ELIGIBLE (open, not draft, base = trunk,
     no hold label, no closing keyword in its description or its commits under watcher verification, PR CI
     green on its current head, approved when required, every ``Depends-on: #N`` landed or earlier in the
     batch and none closed unlanded), up to ``batch_size``, preferring PRs whose changed files do not overlap
     the batch so far. Train k = train k-1 + ``git merge --no-ff <head>`` (a message without the PR title,
     which could carry "Fix #N"); a conflict drops that PR (``queue-conflict``), a PR whose dependency was
     dropped waits, and the batch continues. Push the trains and start their CI.

``verdict`` is the deploy pipeline's LAST job (never run for a cancelled or superseded deploy). For every
open issue labelled ``awaiting-verification`` or ``verification-failed`` whose last landing line names an
ancestor of the deployed commit and none of whose PRs is still in flight (every ``merge-steward: queued PR
#n`` line landed, no queued PR references it): success → comment, tick its ``(post-deploy)`` checklist
items, label ``verified``, then CLOSE it when its counted checklist is complete (else ``needs-closeout``);
failure → ``verification-failed`` + ``needs-rework``. Every claim it returns to the pool is released (the
in-progress label and the assignees removed), because SELECT skips a claimed issue.

USAGE
  python scripts/merge_steward.py [--token-stdin | --token-file F] tick [--run-url URL] [--dry-run]
  python scripts/merge_steward.py [...] verdict --sha SHA | --sha-from-event FILE
         --conclusion success|failure|auto [--run-url URL] [--environment NAME]
         [--gitlab-pipeline ID --deploy-jobs job1,job2]
  python scripts/merge_steward.py deploy-guard --event-file FILE   # GitHub deploy: a 40-hex trunk commit or exit 4
  python scripts/merge_steward.py [...] enqueue <pr> [--allow-pending]      # what `enqueue-pr` runs
  python scripts/merge_steward.py [...] dequeue <pr> [--reason TEXT]
  python scripts/merge_steward.py [...] check-pr <pr>                       # would the queue take it now?
  python scripts/merge_steward.py [...] record-landing <pr>                 # merge "direct": the landing line
  python scripts/merge_steward.py [...] status [--json]
The git wrapper's delivery verbs pass the wrapper's own token with ``--token-stdin``, so an agent session needs
no extra secret. Without a token option the steward reads ``credentials/github-pat.txt`` (GitHub) or the ``token``
of ``config/gitlab.json`` — and the latter only while git ignores that file: a token is never read from a
tracked file. ``steward.cookie_file`` adds an authenticating proxy's cookies to every API call and git push.

EXIT CODES  0 ran (check-pr / enqueue: accepted) · 1 host or git error · 2 usage or configuration error ·
            4 refused (check-pr / enqueue: the PR is not acceptable; the reason is printed)

SECURITY  The tick runs the DEFAULT BRANCH's copy of this file (schedule / dispatch triggers only, never
          ``pull_request``), merges PR content as data and executes none of it (git runs with
          ``core.hooksPath`` pointed at an empty directory and without any inherited ``GIT_DIR``-style
          variable, so a parent git process cannot redirect it), and reads its token from stdin or a file —
          never from a shell variable (``no-environment-vars``). It pushes only branches under
          ``train_prefix`` and fast-forwards of the trunk; it never force-pushes.
"""

